WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:02.149 align:start position:0%
 
we begin in india where calls are  growing for a national lockdown as  covid19 deaths remain  on the rise nearly 3 800 people lost  their lives in a single day  hospitals are struggling to treat  patients as they run out of beds  and oxygen for weeks now volunteer  groups have been delivering crucial  supplies  and dw reporters namisha jaiswal and  sharikh ahmad  have been to meet individuals who are  putting their lives on the line to save  others  dinesh sinha has spent the last two days  right here  on the road in front of a sikh temple 50  kilometers from home  his son has the corona virus and has  also developed a severe lung infection  he urgently needs to be hospitalized but  his father  says even this makeshift arrangement is  a blessing  these people are working 24 hours  providing oxygen cylinders  otherwise it's very difficult to get  oxygen or to get a bed in a hospital  this morning i visited 15 or 20  hospitals to look for a bed for him  there isn't a single bed all of them  refused us  for the last two weeks delhi has been  facing a severe shortage of medical  oxygen  this is a life-saving last resort a free  oxygen service run by khalsa help  a sikh group volunteers here consider  the risks they are taking to be  secondary to the central tenet of  service  in sikhism several of them have already  been infected  but those here tell us they aren't  afraid  they say they must do something to save  these lives  i go home to sleep for two hours  my wife  [Music]  every few minutes critical patients are  brought in  the group has nine trucks scouring  nearby plants for cylinders  the oxygen here is provided for free as  patients continue to be rushed in here  many hospitals in delhi have stopped  taking in new patients  because of the oxygen crisis they're  putting out sos messages on twitter  begging for help as they only have hours  sometimes  minutes of oxygen left this hospital  is one of them the previous night was a  close shave  after waiting for hours at oxygen plants  tagging authorities on twitter  and even reducing oxygen flows to  subsistence levels to make it last  longer  the hospital got supplies in the nick of  time  doctors here tell us that supplies that  used to last them two weeks  now evaporate in 36 hours  they cannot predict if the next round  will reach them on time  turning away patients for logistical  reasons is  heartbreaking for doctors when we refuse  many of these patients are helplessly  pleading  in this profession because is because  they wanted to help other  people through their sickness and  illness  if they are not able to do that that by  itself  is uh taking an emotional moral ethical  toll on them  a rare few do get lucky  back at the oxygen service sinha has  finally managed to arrange a bed for his  son  immediately his cylinder is rolled  towards another patient  two weeks into the oxygen crisis here  every labored breath is a gift  and joining us now to talk more about  volunteer efforts  and helping the sick is inayat singh  kakar she is a health activist with the  people's health movement and she is  joining us from  india thank you so much for being on the  program today  how are you managing to get relief to  the sick when there's so little oxygen  to go around  i mean it's extremely extremely  challenging i mean you know one aspect  is that there is very little oxygen to  go around because  uh you know the daily uh requirement the  daily allocation of delhi  is not even being met and the second  issue is with regards to information  gaps  uh now even you know even in places  where there is oxygen available  it runs out very fast people don't get  to know where it's available where do  you go how do you  cure it uh people you know i mean often  there is  oxygen available in refilling plants but  people don't have empty cylinders  um and so coordinating all of this is  very very difficult  uh but we do have linkages with you know  other volunteer groups with people like  you showed in your story who are  providing oxygen  um and we do our best um you know to get  them oxygen another big challenge is  that  um you know that uh that the prices for  oxygen have really skyrocketed and when  you're dealing with the supplier you  don't really know whether they're  legitimate or whether it's being sold on  the black market  um and that is you know that is a that's  a big  uh problem because also i mean a lot of  people can't afford these extremely high  uh prices so we often also have to  resort to fundraising for individual  patients if uh  you know if that is required we have  been hearing that more foreign aid  is arriving uh foreign help to the  country is that something that you're  noticing  on the ground um and if you're not  noticing it yet i mean how hopeful are  you  for the future prospects in the next  weeks and the next days  um i think a lot of organizations  particularly that are  you know providing oxygen there are  organizations seek organizations  that have set up what are called oxygen  lungers which is basically i mean it's a  place where anyone can come to get free  oxygen  and i think a lot of these organizations  are receiving foreign aid  they you know organizations are flying  in oxygen concentrators  for individuals providing these in their  homes we got to know  uh yesterday that um you know  ventilators  that arrived from abroad have been  distributed to central government  hospitals  so we're hopeful that things will see an  improvement but i'm not sure  if uh you know if our system has worked  fast  enough to address the growing crisis i  feel like we're still very much  you know taking knee-jerk reactions and  we're still trying to fill the shortages  and not really you know we're not really  able to  um to to respond to the crisis  that we're seeing and that we will be  seeing in the coming few days  and as you take that really proactive  approach i mean to fill these gaps in  capacities uh that the system does have  in india  tell us i mean how are you coping on a  day-to-day basis your your organization  yourself uh are you receiving for  example  you know some sort of psychological  support in in dealing with what you're  dealing with every day i mean this is  life and death  uh i mean i think it's extremely  distressing you know a lot of us are not  getting enough sleep  um we're usually working around the  clock there have been days where um you  know some of us have been sleeping once  in 48 hours  um there is a sense of uh you know  despair  uh when you're not able to help patients  uh earlier  you know we were having to help patients  get oxygen at home but now we also get  sos calls from patients who are admitted  in hospitals  um and that's very complicated because  when somebody reaches out to you  for an icu bed you also have to ensure  that the hospital has adequate oxygen  and  you know we're not we don't have access  to system resources to be able to do all  of that  um and i think i don't think a lot of us  are really processing the emotional toll  that this is taking you know a lot of  young volunteers are dealing with cases  where patients are not surviving i think  there is this um you know general  feeling of guilt  for not being able to do everything to  really save the patient and and  i think we're all very very aware of  uh the limitations that we're facing um  we  you know our phone numbers are public we  get constant calls for help we are able  to respond to some people we're not able  to respond to some people  um but but yes we're trying to set up a  more organized form of you know  providing support and psychosocial  support to each other  um and in fact yesterday i spoke to a  therapist you know um  i mean asking her if she can do some  group sessions online for volunteers  um so that we can deal with this  collective trauma that we're all facing  and i also  worry and i also fear for the you know  for the for what's going to happen once  all of this  you know also goes down well you're  doing incredible work uh under difficult  circumstances  and um and and we know that uh you know  your time is also very precious so so  we thank you so much for taking the time  to speak with us here on dw news to tell  us  a little bit about what you're  experiencing there in a volunteer  capacity  in the country as as the death toll  mounts as we mentioned  inia singh kakar health activist  with the people's health movement we  appreciate it thank you  and germany is about to send its second  shipment of emergency aid to  india the air force has already sent a  plane with 120 ventilators and medicine  also on board a team of medical experts  several european states and other  countries including the u.s and taiwan  have also joined  in the aid effort  and benjamin alvarez gruber is standing  by in wunstdorf  air base in lower saxony germany from  where this shipment just took  off uh benjamin what's been happening  near you  that's right sarah we are standing right  next to the runway here at the air force  base of the german  army of the air force just a few minutes  ago the a400m military cargo plane took  off to  abu dhabi to then continue the trip to  delhi as you just said on saturday  another plane landed  already sending a crew that will help to  operate  this oxygen generator machine that is on  board  of this plane there will be a german  team helping their indian colleagues to  use  this machine that compresses air to use  it  for oxygen for several hospitals that  are in dire need  to get in germany joined some 40  countries  offering aid to india um tell us a  little bit more about the efforts and  how far  they might be able to go  that's right germany is joining more  than 40 countries  over the past days there have been a lot  of cargo planes  arriving from australia from the us from  the uk  what is special about this shipment that  germany is standing so we have this  takeoff today we'll have a  takeoff tomorrow as well with the same  plane  a400m airbus cargo transporter  what's interesting is that the german  team will stay there  the first team arrived on saturday they  assessed the situation there  and we talked to several officials  before  this live and they told us that the  security aspect is also very important  if we consider that there is indeed a  black market of oxygen  in the country and also over concerns  that the aid that several countries  are sending out is not reaching the  hospitals and is still in costumes for  example  in airports or in several halls that  have been  put in place by the government so  there's also a fear when we look there's  india media reporting that the aid is  not reaching also other  regions in india so they will be really  careful that this  machine and all the help that is being  sent to india right now can be used as  soon as it arrives there  dw reporter benjamin alvarez kuba thank  you

